title A citation is a promise wherever it is written -- and a fold repoints only what it can see
prompt Autonomous loop -- roster cold first, Standfast on reds, next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first, rota row N mod 5, condense ITINERARY and refill Now, send each finished increment
think Lap index from git log --oneline | wc -l = 3422 at the open, so the council rota reads row 3422 mod 5 = 2 -- Fire and Mars, cut and stop: Standfast, reds-first, and the three depths of removal. The Mars row governed from the middle of the lap onward, because a repair turned into a red and the line stopped on it.
think Opened on my own recommend line: the card is a document meter and says so nowhere, reading F on 30 of 30 planted heredoc fixtures in qa_report_card_control.sh. Measured the class first and it was real -- at least 20 tracked non-prose files carry Markdown link syntax, in three different shapes.
think Measured the obvious fix before writing it and the number killed it. Stripping fenced blocks and backtick spans -- matching what Register and Reach already do -- drops 7 of 30 in one file and 0 in every other, so it would not have fixed either F. Good that the measurement came first; the reasoning had been clean and the answer was still wrong.
think The data pointed elsewhere. A link in pond/customs.rye sits on a //! doc-comment line and resolves; a link in readme_reach_control.sh sits inside a printf that plants a fixture; a link in session_logs_archive.rye sits in a string literal building an index row. The rule the data asks for is that in a program, a citation is a path named in a COMMENT.
think Measured that rule before writing it too: pond/customs.rye keeps 3 of 3, caravan/subscribe_poll_service.rye keeps 3 of 3, session_logs_archive.rye keeps its 2 real ones and drops 28 fragments of the page it writes, and six control scripts drop every planted fixture and keep nothing, which is right, because they cite nothing.
think Scoped it to non-prose deliberately. A Markdown heading begins with # and would read as a comment mark, so applying the comment rule to prose would count only the headings. The fenced-block question reaches 20+ documents and takes its own round rather than riding along on this one.
think Two more clauses came from real cases rather than imagination. lotus/allpass.rye works an example through in a comment -- x[1](32000) + 3/4-y[1](-32768) -- where the brackets are array indices and three targets of 32000, -32768 and 7424 fall out of the pattern, so a target must look like a path. linengrow/murr.rye cites its sibling as ](murr_core.rye) with no slash at all and that citation is real, which is why the rule reads slash OR extension rather than the plainer slash alone.
obs Then the meter found a red, which is the lap's real subject. Four relative citations inside program doc comments name paths that have never existed. tools/rye/session_logs_archive.rye moved one directory deeper in the tools fold of 20260823.144100 and kept the depth its citations were written at, so ](../ORGANIZING.md) points at tools/ORGANIZING.md. Two more broke when a design note folded into active-designing/yonder/.
obs Nothing standing could have caught them, and that is the transferable part. dated_path_repoint and tool_path_repoint move path literals in living code; tracked_link_scan reads links inside .md. A Markdown link inside a .rye comment falls between all three and stood two days. Booked as REDS %218 with the pin folded to hold it.
obs The near-miss is the larger half of the lesson. The first measurement read 15 broken across 11 files, and I was one sed away from repairing them. Eleven were pond/apps/** at mode 120000 -- symlinks onto bodies at pond/, granary/, linengrow/ and mandi/ whose citations resolve exactly right AT THE BODY. A depth-fix sweep would have written through the links into six correct files and broken all six at their real homes.
obs What stopped it was checking whether the files were symlinks before editing them, which is REDS %213's lesson arriving in different clothes: read the data before acting on the instruction, especially when the instruction looks mechanical. The cheapest-looking sweep was again the only one that could have done harm.
obs So the card resolves a link now and reads the citation from where it was written, naming path_kind=symlink so a reader knows which of two doors was measured. Proven from both sides: a symlinked door reads the body clean, and breaking the body is still seen through the door, so resolution is a correction rather than an exemption.
obs A lantern that fired four times became a loom. tools/fixtures/comment_citation_scan.sh over tools/co/comment_citation_witness.rish is seated on the standing roster and gated at zero -- 89 programs, 0 broken, 15 seconds -- reading THROUGH the report card rather than spelling the rule again beside it, so what counts as a citation is decided in one place. Gated rather than ratcheted because the population is small and closed; there is nothing to migrate.
obs The new guard bit me on its first run, and it was right. My own control planted //! comment lines in heredocs, and a heredoc line beginning //! is a comment line in the control file too, with a relative target that resolves from the pen rather than from tools/fixtures. Rewrote the plants through printf so the control's own source stops making a citation it does not mean. An exemption for *_control.sh would have been a door beside the wall.
obs Caught one vacuous plant before it shipped, for the second time this session. The symlink reading in the scan's control passed while testing nothing, because git grep lists the body alone and never the symlink -- so the door was never scanned. Replaced it with what is actually true and worth holding: a body is listed once, so a break is reported once against the body rather than once per door. The card's own control is where the symlink resolution is proven, because there the card can be called on the link's own path.
obs Swept tools/s/session_logs_index_prepend.rish on touch: its usage example now shows a placeholder filename shape rather than a bare file.bron, which is both more honest documentation and one fewer false citation, and its comment em-dashes went to ASCII. Its assert strings keep their bytes, per the prior lap's ruling that the sweep touches comments and leaves strings.
obs Named what I did not do. The ASCII comment guard reads *.rye alone, so .rish and .sh comments sit outside it -- an honest gap rather than a red, since the guard never claimed them. On ITINERARY, with the fenced-block question beside it.
obs Three more guards red on the hot roster, and two were the ordinary cost of adding a witness -- readme_metrics and geode_libraries count witnesses and modules, and the pre-commit hook regenerates both at commit. Regenerated them by hand instead so the roster measures the tree the commit ships rather than the tree before it: 1,684 witnesses to 1,685, 39 library rows.
obs The third was mine and it is a small delight. declared_ceiling counts every tool line that spells the seated living-pin bound, and my planted math example had copied lotus/allpass.rye verbatim -- whose arithmetic works out to a difference equal to that bound. So a control proving one rule tripped an unrelated ratchet by transcription. Gave the plant its own values: a plant carries a SHAPE rather than a transcription. Then did it twice more, because the comment EXPLAINING the change spelled the number as well, and the third pass found the explanation sitting inside the heredoc and writing itself into the pen file.
